  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia provided technical advice to the Philippine government to facilitate the passage of the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP) agreement that will promote trade and market access within the Indo-Pacific region. In 2023, the Philippines was one of the first signatories to ratify the agreement. Through Coalitions for Change (CfC), Foundation for Economic Freedom (FEF) supported the Senate&#x2019;s technical working group and participated in hearings on the RCEP to make a stronger case for its ratification. FEF highlighted the economic benefits of the agreement and provided advice on the impact for the Philippines&apos; agriculture sector.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported a policy that introduced satellite broadband, a cost-effective and ground-breaking technology, to improve internet access. Due to the pandemic, there was a pivot to digital services such as online learning, remote banking, and telemedicine. CfC proposed amendments and introduced a policy that removed restrictions for internet service providers to provide satellite broadband services. As a result, there are now over 20 registered satellite service providers in the country that can service households and institutions that want to access the internet. The policy is expected to increase internet connectivity and improve access to digital services.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported expert advice to the Philippine government to craft policies for economic recovery after COVID-19. To generate revenues and increase investments for long-term growth, CfC provided technical assistance to amend the Public Services Act. The amendment removed foreign ownership restrictions in key sectors to encourage investments, promote competition, and improve service delivery. This can also deepen economic relations between Australia and the Philippines. CfC&#x2019;s partner, Foundation for Economic Freedom (FEF), was successful in inserting national security provisions and was recognised by Congress for their assistance. FEF is also assisting in the development of the law&#x2019;s implementing rules and regulations.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported the Philippine government to enhance its response to cases of violence against women and children (VAWC) that were made more difficult due to strict lockdown measures. CfC worked with government partners that mainstreamed support for gender-based violence (GBV) through the hotline. Telecommunicators refer callers to government services such as rescue, counselling, medical services, and legal support. CfC partners equipped the Emergency 911 National Office to train telecommunicators in implementing this policy. Since the launch of the hotline in 2021, 63.4% (1,100) of the cases have been actioned by the appropriate national agencies.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The BARMM will hold its first parliamentary elections in 2025. From 2019 to 2024, Australia provided technical advice to the BARMM Government through the Coalitions for Change (CfC) program to develop its electoral code and implement rules and regulations. Women hold 20 percent of seats in the BARMM Parliament and high illiteracy for both men and women is a barrier to electoral participation. The Legal Network for Truthful Elections, a CfC partner, provided technical expertise to the BARMM authorities to ensure at least 30 per cent of political party nominees are women. CfC&#x2019;s program also resulted in an improved ballot design to include party and candidate photos which make it more accessible.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
